<span id="S4.T1.3.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">A simple one-line prompt instructing LLMs to rewrite the input to mimic the authorship style of the target exemplar.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.6pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T1.3.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">STYLL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patel et al., 2024</a>)</cite></span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:284.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">An example-based arbitrary TST method. It first prompts the model to rewrite the input into a neutral style, then to describe the target style using a list of style descriptors, and finally to rewrite the neutral rewrite of input using the style descriptors.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
